<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Sir Winston Churchill dies aged 90</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s wartime Prime Minister died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, seventy years to the day after his father. He received a state funeral, the first for a politician since Gladstone.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-15">15 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Canada raises its Maple Leaf flag</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The red and white Maple Leaf flag was raised on Parliament Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a>, replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canadian-red-ensign">Canadian Red Ensign</a>. 15 February is now marked as National Flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> Day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Malcolm X assassinated in Harlem</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-x">Malcolm X</a> was shot dead as he began speaking at the Audubon Ballroom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. A year after leaving the Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, he had been moving towards a broader vision of human rights.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">'Bloody Sunday' on the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> state troopers attacked about 600 civil rights marchers with clubs and tear gas. Televised images shocked the nation and helped secure the Voting Rights Act that August.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-18">18 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Alexei Leonov makes the first spacewalk</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Soviet cosmonaut spent about 12 minutes outside Voskhod 2. His suit ballooned in the vacuum, and he had to let out air to squeeze back into the airlock.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-23">23 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Gemini 3 makes the first crewed Gemini flight</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Gus Grissom and John Young flew three orbits and became the first astronauts to change their spacecraft's orbit. The Gemini programme prepared the techniques for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lyndon Johnson signs Medicare and Medicaid into law</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Johnson signed the Social Security Amendments in Independence, Missouri, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry Truman</a> beside him. Medicare and Medicaid brought health insurance to millions of older and poorer Americans.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Watts riots erupt in Los Angeles</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A traffic stop in the Watts district sparked six days of unrest in which 34 people died. The riots exposed deep anger over poverty and policing in America's segregated cities.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Paul VI becomes the first pope to visit the United States</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Pope Paul VI flew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> for a one-day visit, the first by a reigning pope to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He appealed for peace at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> and celebrated Mass at Yankee Stadium.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-11">11 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Rhodesia declares independence from Britain</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a>'s white minority government issued a Unilateral Declaration of Independence to avoid majority rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the UN imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, and after a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> became independent in 1980.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vietnam-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Battle of Ia Drang begins in Vietnam's central highlands</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The first major battle between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam">People's Army of Vietnam</a> began at the foot of the Chu Pong Massif. It saw the first large-scale <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> air assault and the first tactical use of B-52 bombers.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">France launches its first satellite</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A French Diamant rocket launched the satellite Astérix from Hammaguir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> became the third country, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, to put a satellite into orbit on its own rocket.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches">Selma to Montgomery marches</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Selma to Montgomery marches were three protest marches for African American voting rights, held in 1965 along the 54-mile (87 km) highway from Selma, Alabama, to Montgomery, the state capital.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/unilateral-declaration-of-independence-rhodesia" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/unilateral-declaration-of-independence-rhodesia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Crisis</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/unilateral-declaration-of-independence-rhodesia">Unilateral Declaration of Independence (Rhodesia)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Unilateral Declaration of Independence was issued on 11 November 1965 by Ian Smith's Rhodesian Front government of Southern Rhodesia, which refused Britain's demand for progress to majority rule.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965">Indo-Pakistani War of 1965</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Indo-Pakistani War of 1965 was fought between India and Pakistan from August to September 1965.</p></div></li></ol></section>
